net/sched: sch_cake: cake_mq demand flag armed at the shaper refusal

999-719 ported Segalmen's 714 verbatim, including its non-v1 saturation
criterion:

  sat = throttled && sch->q.qlen;

That term cannot do what its comment says. The measurement runs inside
cake_dequeue, after the head guard "if (!sch->q.qlen) return NULL;", and
sch->q.qlen is only decremented later, by cake_dequeue_one: qlen is >= 1
for the whole sync block. The term is therefore always true and the
criterion collapses to "throttled".

It also cannot be repaired in place. overlimits has exactly two
increment sites in the file:

  cake_enqueue, under "if (!sch->q.qlen)"   the queue is EMPTY
  cake_dequeue, at begin:                   the shaper refuses, the packet

The counter freely mixes those two, and they are exactly the two cases
the term was meant to separate. The discriminating information -- enqueue
or dequeue -- is not in the queue length at the sync point, where the
packet about to be served is still queued. No test on qlen can recover
it.

Add the signal at the place where it is true. The dequeue refusal is the
one event that means "the instance's cap bit while a backlog was still
queued", so arm a per-instance flag there and let the sync block consume
it:

  - q->rb_demand is set at the global hard shaper refusal, the site that
    returns NULL with the packet still queued;
  - the tick winner reads it and clears it, exactly as it already handles
    rb_bytes and rb_overlimits;
  - the non-v1 branch uses it in place of "throttled && sch->q.qlen".

The flag accumulates over the ticks an instance does not win, so it reads
as "refused at least once since this instance last won a tick". No ageing
is needed on the peer side: the existing activity test already excludes
instances that have not sent within g_active_window_ns, so a flag left
armed by an instance that has gone quiet is never consulted.

An instance that truly is capped always refuses at that site, so there
are no false negatives; the false positives of the old term are removed,
because the enqueue site -- which fires only on an EMPTY queue, the exact
opposite of "with a backlog" -- no longer contributes.

This changes behaviour in two places, both intended:

  - mode 0: the "saturated" counter no longer counts an overlimit that
    came from the enqueue path.
  - mode 2: saturated instances are the ones actually refused with work
    left, so num_sat/num_light -- and hence the redistributed spare --
    are no longer computed from an always-true test.
  Mode 1 is untouched: it keeps the served >= cap - cap>>sat_shift test.

Observable fact, verifiable without a rebuild: cake_mq/stats gains a
"demand" row per direction, counting the syncs where the winner found its
flag armed. It is the discriminant that separates the two ways this can
fail silently -- a flag that never fires (demand stays 0, saturated
collapses to 0) cannot be told from a criterion that is merely never
satisfied without it. rb_demand starts at zero because the qdisc private
area comes from qdisc_alloc's kzalloc, the same assumption 999-714
already relies on for rb_bytes and rb_overlimits.

Requires 999-718 and 999-719. Not built, not flashed. Local port only.

--- a/net/sched/sch_cake.c
+++ b/net/sched/sch_cake.c
@@ -133,6 +133,7 @@
 	atomic64_t nact[CAKE_MQ_NACT_BINS];	/* files actives 1..8+ */
 	atomic64_t nact_changes;		/* le compte a change */
 	atomic64_t saturated;			/* instance saturee a ce tick */
+	atomic64_t demand;			/* syncs ou le drapeau de demande etait pose */
 	atomic64_t rate_kbs_sum;		/* somme debit local, KiB/s */
 	atomic64_t overcommit;			/* syncs ou la somme des caps > debit global */
 	atomic64_t overcommit_kbs_sum;		/* exces sur le debit global, KiB/s */
@@ -361,6 +362,7 @@
 	u64		rb_cap;		/* debit local applique au dernier tick, octets/s */
 	u32		rb_overlimits;	/* overlimits au dernier tick */
 	u8		rb_saturated;	/* bridage shaper pendant le dernier intervalle */
+	u8		rb_demand;	/* shaper en tete, avec du backlog en file */
 
 	/* partage des tins (999-715) */
 	u64		tin_last_active[CAKE_MAX_TINS];	/* dernier dequeue par tin */
@@ -2269,6 +2271,7 @@
 					u64 cap = q->rb_cap ? q->rb_cap : rate;
 					u32 ol = sch->qstats.overlimits;
 					bool throttled = ol != q->rb_overlimits;
+					u8 demand = READ_ONCE(q->rb_demand);
 					u64 r = 0;
 					u8 sat;
 
@@ -2281,10 +2284,16 @@
 						sat = throttled &&
 						      r >= cap - (cap >> sat_shift);
 					else
-						/* v2 et amont : bride avec du
-						 * backlog encore en file
+						/* v2 et amont : le drapeau pose
+						 * par le refus du shaper, pas
+						 * l'ancien test sur qlen qui
+						 * valait toujours vrai ici.
 						 */
-						sat = throttled && sch->q.qlen;
+						sat = demand;
+					WRITE_ONCE(q->rb_demand, 0);
+					if (demand)
+						atomic64_inc(&g_mq_stats[
+							!!(cfg->rate_flags & CAKE_FLAG_INGRESS)].demand);
 					q->rb_bytes = 0;
 					q->rb_overlimits = ol;
 					WRITE_ONCE(q->rb_rate, cake_ewma(q->rb_rate, r, ewma_shift));
@@ -2458,6 +2467,14 @@
 		u64 next = min(ktime_to_ns(q->time_next_packet),
 			       ktime_to_ns(q->failsafe_next_packet));
 
+		/* Ici et seulement ici : le shaper d'instance refuse, et le
+		 * paquet reste en file (la garde de tete a etabli qlen >= 1).
+		 * Le compteur overlimits ne distingue pas ce cas de celui de
+		 * cake_enqueue, qui n'arrive que sur une file VIDE : ce
+		 * drapeau est le seul signal qui porte le "avec backlog" que
+		 * le bloc de synchro ne peut pas lire dans qlen.
+		 */
+		WRITE_ONCE(q->rb_demand, 1);
 		sch->qstats.overlimits++;
 		qdisc_watchdog_schedule_ns(&q->watchdog, next);
 		return NULL;
@@ -3777,6 +3794,8 @@
 		seq_printf(m, "%s: overcommit %lld overcommit_kbs_sum %lld\n",
 			   dir[d], (s64)atomic64_read(&st->overcommit),
 			   (s64)atomic64_read(&st->overcommit_kbs_sum));
+		seq_printf(m, "%s: demand %lld\n", dir[d],
+			   (s64)atomic64_read(&st->demand));
 		seq_printf(m, "%s: nact", dir[d]);
 		for (i = 0; i < CAKE_MQ_NACT_BINS; i++)
 			seq_printf(m, " %lld", (s64)atomic64_read(&st->nact[i]));
